.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH qsh-pair-accept 1  "accept 0.6.0" 
.SH NAME
qsh\-pair\-accept \- Dial `address`, redeem `code` against its invite, and — on a successful mutual proof — pin the peer exactly as `trust add` would (ADR\-0002, `docs/CLI.md` §6.11)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBqsh pair accept\fR [\fB\-\-code\-stdin\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-as\fR] [\fB\-\-jsonl\fR] [\fB\-\-role\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR] [\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-punch\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-iroh\fR] [\fB\-\-rendezvous\fR] [\fB\-\-jump\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIADDRESS\fR> [\fICODE\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Dial `address`, redeem `code` against its invite, and — on a successful mutual proof — pin the peer exactly as `trust add` would (ADR\-0002, `docs/CLI.md` §6.11)
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-code\-stdin\fR
Read the invite code from standard input, to end of input, ignoring leading and trailing whitespace. Use this instead of the positional code to keep it out of shell history.

If standard input is a terminal, echo is suppressed the same way it is for the prompt, and `\-\-json`/`\-\-jsonl` still refuses rather than waiting on it: pipe or redirect the code in for machine mode.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit a single `qsh.cli/v1` JSON envelope on stdout instead of human\-readable text
.TP
\fB\-\-as\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Pin the redeemed peer under this name instead of its self\- asserted one (`docs/CLI.md` §6.11, ADR\-0012 decision 6). When omitted in human mode, the pinned self\-asserted name is printed after the pin succeeds, along with a suggested label derived from `address` when it has a hostname (never for an IP literal)
.TP
\fB\-\-jsonl\fR
Emit newline\-delimited JSON on stdout (reserved for streaming commands; non\-streaming commands emit a single line, same as `\-\-json`)
.TP
\fB\-\-role\fR \fI<ROLE>\fR
Trust role for the pin written here: one of `full`, `exec`, `tunnel`, `read\-only`, `reverse`, `none` (default `none`: the pin is a peer to dial, `docs/CLI.md` §6.11, ADR\-0045)
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase diagnostic verbosity on stderr (`\-v` info, `\-vv` debug, `\-vvv` trace). Never affects stdout (`docs/CLI.md` §2.2)
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress diagnostic output on stderr
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-ssh\fR
Never run ssh on this command\*(Aqs behalf: no automatic bootstrap of an unknown name, and no restarting of a pinned host\*(Aqs serve over ssh when the connection fails (`docs/CLI.md` §7). `qsh pair ssh` is not affected; it is ssh by definition
.TP
\fB\-\-install\-remote\fR
When the ssh bootstrap finds no `qsh` on the remote host (or one too old to answer it), install this machine\*(Aqs release into `~/.local/bin` there without asking (`docs/CLI.md` §7). Works with `\-\-json`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-install\-remote\fR
Never install or upgrade `qsh` on the remote host, and do not ask; a missing `qsh` ends as `remote_qsh_missing`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-punch\fR
Never start a hole punch on this command\*(Aqs behalf, and send no `PunchRequest` (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0054 decision 19). The route is the direct dial alone. Wins over `[punch].mode`
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-iroh\fR
Never take the iroh relay route on this command, whatever the host\*(Aqs pin `routes` and `[iroh].mode` say, and contact no relay (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0061 decision 1). Not stored
.TP
\fB\-\-rendezvous\fR \fI<PEER>\fR
Use this pinned peer as the rendezvous for hole punching on this command, instead of the host\*(Aqs own `rendezvous` hints (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0054 decision 4). Must be pinned with an address. Not stored
.TP
\fB\-\-jump\fR \fI<HOPS>\fR
Reach the host through these pinned peers, first hop first, for this command: `a` or `a,b` (`docs/CLI.md` §7, ADR\-0056 decision 20). Each hop is the name of a pin; a `user@` or `:port` on it is ignored by the QUIC path. `none` goes the ordinary way even when the host\*(Aqs pin has a `jump`. Wins over `\-o ProxyJump` and over the pin\*(Aqs own `jump`. Not stored. The short `\-J` is the same flag on the commands that take it
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fIADDRESS\fR>
`host:port` of the device that printed `code` via `pair invite`. With no `:port`, port 4433 is assumed
.TP
[\fICODE\fR]
The invite code, as printed (case\-insensitive, hyphens ignored).

Optional. With no code here and no `\-\-code\-stdin`, a terminal is prompted for it with echo off, and `\-\-json`/`\-\-jsonl` returns INVALID_ARGUMENT instead of prompting.

A fingerprint is a public value, so `trust add \-\-fingerprint` left in shell history is not a risk. A code left in history has no reuse value either: an invite is single\-use and stops being redeemable 10 minutes after `pair invite` mints it.
.SH VERSION
v0.6.0
